-- title:
--   Theorem 1, p. 10 — under Assumption 1 the risk-sensitive mean-field game admits a mean-field equilibrium
-- statement:
--   Throughout, $\mathsf X$ and $\mathsf A$ are Polish spaces with their Borel σ-fields, $\mathsf A$ is compact and nonempty, $(\mathsf X,\mathsf A,p,c,\mu_0)$ is a mean-field game model with discount factor $\beta\in(0,1)$, risk factor $\lambda>0$ and a measurable cost $c\ge 0$, and Assumption 1 holds: $c$ is continuous with $\|c\|\le K$, $p$ is weakly continuous in $(x,a,\mu)$, and there are $\alpha\ge0$ and a continuous moment function $w\ge1$ with $\int w\,dp(\cdot|x,a,\mu)\le\alpha w(x)$ and $\int w\,d\mu_0<\infty$.
--
--   Then the mean-field game $(\mathsf X,\mathsf A,p,c,\mu_0)$ admits a mean-field equilibrium: there are a Markov policy $\pi$ and a measure flow $\mu=(\mu_t)_{t\ge0}$ such that
--   $$\mu=\Lambda(\pi)\qquad\text{and}\qquad J_\mu(\pi)=\inf_{\sigma\in\Pi}J_\mu(\sigma),$$
--   where $\Lambda(\pi)$ is the flow of state distributions generated by $\pi$ from $\mu_0$, $\Pi$ is the set of all history-dependent policies and
--   $$J_\mu(\sigma)=E^\sigma\Big[\exp\Big(\lambda\sum_{t=0}^\infty\beta^tc(x(t),a(t),\mu_t)\Big)\Big].$$
--
--   This is the existence half of the paper; the other main result (Theorem 3, the subject of the second mission of this series) shows that the equilibrium policy is approximately Nash in every large enough finite-population game.
--
--   **Formalization Note** The conclusion asks for a **Markov** equilibrium policy that is optimal against **all history-dependent** policies, which is at least as strong as Definition 3 (Markov policies are policies, and on a Markov policy $\Lambda$ is the explicit recursion of p. 9); the paper's proof produces such a policy (Proposition 3 disintegrates a fixed point of $\Gamma$ into a Markov policy; Theorem 2 and Proposition 1 give optimality over all policies). The standing assumptions of the paper's §3–§4 (Polish $\mathsf X$, $\mathsf A$; $\beta\in(0,1)$, $\lambda>0$, $c\ge0$ measurable; Assumption 1) are hypotheses. Nonemptiness of $\mathsf A$ is added: the paper uses it tacitly (with $\mathsf A=\emptyset$ there is no policy at all).
-- source:
--   Saldi, Başar & Raginsky, Discrete-time Risk-sensitive Mean-field Games, arXiv:1808.03929v2, p. 10, Theorem 1; proof §4, pp. 10–20

import Mathlib
import Definitions.Def_RiskSensMFG_Existence_Model
import Definitions.Def_RiskSensMFG_Existence_Equilibrium

open MeasureTheory ProbabilityTheory

namespace RiskSensMFG.Existence

/-- Theorem 1 (p. 10): under Assumption 1 the mean-field game `(X, A, p, c, μ₀)` admits a
mean-field equilibrium `(π, μ)`, with `π` a Markov policy optimal among all policies. -/
theorem theorem_1 {X A : Type*} [TopologicalSpace X] [PolishSpace X] [MeasurableSpace X] [BorelSpace X]
    [TopologicalSpace A] [PolishSpace A] [MeasurableSpace A] [BorelSpace A] [CompactSpace A]
    [Nonempty A] (M : Model X A) (hS : M.Standing) (h1 : Assumption1 M) :
    ∃ (π : MarkovPolicy X A) (μ : ℕ → PM X), IsMFE M π μ := by sorry

end RiskSensMFG.Existence
